'''''Eldonia''''' is an extinct soft-bodied [[cambroernid]] animal of unknown affinity,<ref>{{harvnb|Signor|Vermeij|1994|page=302}}</ref> best known from the Fossil Ridge outcrops of the [[Burgess Shale]], particularly in the 'Great ''Eldonia'' layer' in the [[Walcott Quarry]].<ref name='Gabbott2008'>{{harvnb|Gabbott|Zalasiewicz|Collins|2008|page=311, 316–317}}</ref> In addition to over 550 specimens collected by Walcott,<ref>{{harvnb|Durham|1974|page=752}}</ref> 224 specimens of ''Eldonia'' are known from the Greater [[Phyllopod bed]], where they comprise 0.43% of the community.{{citation needed|date=December 2024 |reason=Previously cited Caron, Jean-Bernard; Jackson, Donald A. (October 2006). "Taphonomy of the Greater Phyllopod Bed community, Burgess Shale". PALAIOS. 21 (5): 451–65. Bibcode:2006Palai..21..451C. doi:10.2110/palo.2003.P05-070R. JSTOR 20173022. S2CID 53646959 https://jackson.eeb.utoronto.ca/files/2012/10/Caron-and-Jackson-2006-Palaois.pdf but I am unable to find this information in the text.}}  Species also occur in the Chengjiang biota,<ref name=Chen1995>{{harvnb|Chen|Zhu|Zhou|1995|page=213}}</ref> Siberia,<ref>{{harvnb|Friend|Zhuravlev|Solov’ev|2002|page=463}}</ref> and in Upper [[Ordovician]] strata of [[Morocco]].

A specimen from the [[Lower Ordovician]] [[Madaoyu Formation]] in [[Hunan]], South China, can be interpreted as the incomplete body of ''Eldonia'' or the similar animal. However, its annulation, the structure of the [[intestine]] and the shape of the body are more similar to those of ''[[Ottoia]]''.<ref name=Fang2022>{{harvnb|Fang|Mao|Liu|Yuan|2022|page=5}}</ref>

== Description ==
It takes the form of a round, medusoid disk (which originally led to suggestions of a jellyfish affinity)<ref name=Chen1995 /> with a C-shaped gut trace.  The gut is recalcitrant and can be extracted using [[Hydrofluoric acid]].<ref name=Butterfield1990>{{harvnb|Butterfield|1990}}</ref> 

== Classification ==
Walcott's original interpretation as a [[holothurian]] was rapidly disputed.<ref>{{harvnb|Clark|1912|pages=276–278}}</ref>  Alternative affinities to be suggested, which did not stand the test of time, included the [[siphonophores]]<ref>{{harvnb|Madsen|1957|page=281}}</ref><ref>{{harvnb|Madsen|1962}}</ref><ref>{{harvnb|Madsen|1956}}</ref> and a [[Coelenterata|coelenterate]] [[medusa (biology)|medusa]].<ref>{{harvnb|Henning|1960}}</ref>

''Eldonia'' and the other Eldoniidae form a clade, [[Eldonioidea]], with the [[Rotadiscidae]] and the informal group known as the [[paropsonemid]]s.<ref>{{harvnb|Schroeder|Paterson|Brock|2018|page=80–81}}</ref>  The Eldonioidea are, in turn, part of the stem-[[ambulacrarian]] clade [[Cambroernida]].<ref>{{harvnb|Li|Dunn|Murdock|Guo|2023|pages=2362–2364}}</ref>

== Paleoecology ==
The organism is frequently found in association with the lobopod ''[[Microdictyon]]'', which is presumed to have fed on ''Eldonia''.<ref name='Zhang2007'>{{harvnb|Zhang|Hou|2007|pages=448–449}}</ref>

